1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
Tim Hawks:

2
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:09.998
So, it is going to be one of those mornings when driving into work is kind of frustrating because of the wet weather out there.

3
00:00:09.998 --> 00:00:27.309
And while you are kind of talking negatively under your breath, I just want to remind you guys that a little less than two years ago, we were facing a drought that paralleled some of the worst droughts ever in the history of Central Texas.

4
00:00:27.309 --> 00:00:37.159
And a group of pastors decided that we wanted to start praying for rain in our city and to fill the lakes back up and to make it public.

5
00:00:37.159 --> 00:00:52.586
A group of guys went down, pastors went down to the Austin city council and got on the docket and got up and told the city council that we were declaring a day of prayer and fasting for rain, and prayed over that.

6
00:00:52.586 --> 00:01:06.710
And of course, you know, it is Austin, so you can imagine what we got out of that. But the mayor actually signed a little proclamation that he acknowledged that the pastors were praying for rain.

7
00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:15.329
Not that the city was, or not that the city was involved in it. It was just acknowledged that the pastors were praying.

8
00:01:15.329 --> 00:01:28.946
Anyway, here we sit less than two years later and Lake Travis is at over 681, full to what is normal capacity, and Lake Buchanan has come up. We are really, really close to total capacity.

9
00:01:28.946 --> 00:01:40.824
So, tomorrow night at Hyde Park Baptist Church, there is a praise gathering, a prayer and praise gathering like we had a year ago or two years ago to pray for rain.

10
00:01:40.824 --> 00:02:02.046
And so, for those of you guys who maybe have been praying for rain, maybe you put your boat in the water, maybe you have a business that sells boats to people or fishing lures or whatever it is, or you just want to thank God publicly for what he has done, that would be an awesome thing to do.

11
00:02:02.046 --> 00:02:17.256
So, just let you know, tomorrow night at Hyde Park Baptist Church. I am not going to actually be able to be there because we kick off our neighborhood group tomorrow night. And so we are going to be doing that.

12
00:02:17.256 --> 00:02:20.225
So I want to make you guys aware of that.

13
00:02:20.225 --> 00:02:36.087
Second thing to make you aware of is that there is an apologetics conference called Questions Next Door that is coming to Austin the 8th and the 9th. That is next Friday and Saturday, not this weekend, but next weekend.

14
00:02:36.087 --> 00:02:51.732
And if you guys are interested in that, some of the classic apologetic speakers are going to be there. Guys like Hugh Ross, scientist on the origins of the earth and so forth. And then a couple other really key guys.

15
00:02:51.732 --> 00:03:04.914
And then several pastors really answering the questions that you tend to run into at work or with your neighbors, the people around you. I am going to be doing my two talks on Islam.

16
00:03:04.914 --> 00:03:09.622
So, if you guys missed those talks, I will be talking about that.

17
00:03:09.622 --> 00:04:04.380
And I am also going to be doing my talk on science and Christianity and the history of science and Christianity, overturning the myths that have been perpetuated about this kind of war between science and Christianity which, once you understand what is actually happening in history instead of the fabrication that you are getting in your education system and so forth, it is amazing to understand how science grew in the West because it came out of the concept of reason that is embedded in Christianity, which is totally different than what the rest of the world was embracing, and that is why science developed in the West under Christianity, actually in and through the church, instead of the other parts of the world where it actually did not develop.

18
00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:19.445
So I will be doing my talk on that. But there will be a bunch of other guys speaking. And it will be really good. So Questions Next Door is the website if you guys are interested, want to go and get on there.

19
00:04:19.445 --> 00:04:26.470
Today we are talking about a man and his sons. Last week we talked about a man and his daughters.

20
00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:38.783
And so today we are going to walk through how do we raise our sons in a way that they embrace and understand what it means to be a man and the things that make that easy.

21
00:04:38.783 --> 00:04:53.052
And what I would challenge you guys on is to continue to work on your manhood plan. We only have a few more weeks and we will be wrapping up our talk. And so I want you to continue to work on that.

22
00:04:53.052 --> 00:04:59.643
You say, I am still not sure what that is all about. Check with Nate. Nate can fill you in.

23
00:04:59.643 --> 00:05:11.232
We have got a plan that kind of helps you walk through the questions to answer, but really challenge you and encourage you to keep working on your manhood plan.

24
00:05:11.232 --> 00:05:26.297
If you are not planning to go somewhere, you are not planning to go anywhere. And we know that that is the case from most of the important things in our life. We set out a plan and we plan to do those things.

25
00:05:26.297 --> 00:05:40.203
And in the failure to plan, you just do not go anywhere. And so we challenge you that without a plan, you are just depending on God to come through for you and you are not really contributing.

26
00:05:40.203 --> 00:05:53.747
So when it comes to raising a son, the challenge is that each of us would have a plan in mind for how we would raise our sons to maturity. And we are going to unpack a little bit of that.

27
00:05:53.747 --> 00:06:09.392
The reason why we need a plan for our sons is three-fold. First of all, because of depravity, our own internal issues. In other words, we recognize the limitations we have in our masculinity. That is why we are here.

28
00:06:09.392 --> 00:06:21.125
And so if we do not deal with our internal depravity and have a plan to elevate our sons: how many of you want your sons to be less than you or even equal to you?

29
00:06:21.125 --> 00:06:35.901
Do you not want your sons to thrive better than you, to go further than you, to do more than you? And so if you just kind of rock along, your own internal problems will get in the way of raising your son.

30
00:06:35.901 --> 00:06:47.562
We also have the external issues. That is the culture around. The culture around presents a different model of manhood, a model of manhood that is self-centered.

31
00:06:47.562 --> 00:07:01.758
And your sons are going to pick up on that every single day. They are being told that. And so if you do not have a plan, you are working against culture. The third is you are working against time.

32
00:07:01.758 --> 00:07:10.667
You only have a finite amount of time to train, coach, and lead your sons through the experiences of their formative years.

33
00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:26.094
And so to go years and years and years with your head down focusing on your stuff and not paying attention to what your son needs from you, that is a dangerous place to be. You do not have that much time with him.

34
00:07:26.094 --> 00:07:33.192
I also would challenge us today that I think that we believe that proximity is equal to parenting.

35
00:07:33.192 --> 00:07:46.809
And so therefore, if I am sitting within a hundred feet of my son on the sidelines watching them play in their sports or whatever all day long, that somehow I have been a father to my son.

36
00:07:46.809 --> 00:08:02.599
We can confuse this proximity idea because he is riding in the car from me, falling asleep, or playing his video game while we are driving him from activity to activity to activity. And I am considering that parenting.

37
00:08:02.599 --> 00:08:06.365
I have missed it because proximity is not parenting.

38
00:08:06.365 --> 00:08:22.155
I also want to remind you guys that kids activities has become big business. There are economic forces out there telling you that if you are going to be a good dad, you need to get your kids in all of these activities.

39
00:08:22.155 --> 00:08:38.090
Same idea that tells you that you are not really a man unless you are driving a more expensive car than you are driving, living in a nicer house than you are living in, having more amenities. It is market-driven culture.

40
00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:52.286
And so just be careful with that. Be thoughtful about how much time your child needs to be involved in various things and how much time they need to not be. How much time they need to be with you.

41
00:08:52.286 --> 00:09:01.557
How much time they need to be doing nothing so that they can develop some other skills like listening and learning and relating.

42
00:09:01.557 --> 00:09:16.477
You say, well, yeah, but if my kid is not playing sports or doing whatever, he is playing video games. Whose fault is that? Do not buy the stupid things. Do not provide them. Do not put them in their hands.

43
00:09:16.477 --> 00:09:28.138
How many of you all when you were a kid growing up, you had a smartphone? How many of you, because you did not have a smartphone, you got kidnapped and murdered?

44
00:09:28.138 --> 00:09:41.682
This whole marketing thing that if you really love your child, you want your child to be safe is making Apple rich. Now, some of you are working for Apple. You can smile like that, right?

45
00:09:41.682 --> 00:09:57.617
But I need to know where my kid is because it is a dangerous world out there. And so therefore, I need to put something in their hand that they are going to use to go mindless for hours and hours and hours on end. Right?

46
00:09:57.617 --> 00:10:02.904
Think about it, gentlemen. Okay. So that is it. I am done with that part.

47
00:10:02.904 --> 00:10:17.028
So let us talk about volleyball. I have a lot of experience with volleyball because I spent a lot of time with my daughter who played D1 volleyball. And she is the tall, mean-looking one on here.

48
00:10:17.028 --> 00:10:32.818
You probably cannot see her, but anyway. Spent a lot of time playing volleyball with my daughter. And if we had time, we could talk a whole lot about what we did and what commitments she had to make to be able to play.

49
00:10:32.818 --> 00:10:45.058
And they included being in church on Sunday. And they included that she was going to get her stuff done. And they included that we were going to be together as a family.

50
00:10:45.058 --> 00:10:56.067
And they also included that we were not coming to all of those games and all those tournaments because we had three other kids and we have a life to do.

51
00:10:56.067 --> 00:11:04.541
So she had to work with that sport within the context of the confines. But anyway, I love the sport. I love the game.

52
00:11:04.541 --> 00:11:12.218
And we are going to build this talk around these three things that really make up a volleyball experience.

53
00:11:12.218 --> 00:11:22.793
The first one is the dig. And for those of you guys who have played, you know that the most important thing is serve receive. The dig or the pass.

54
00:11:22.793 --> 00:11:36.917
Because if the person who receives the ball coming over the net cannot get down low enough and put the ball in play for the setter, you are going to get nowhere. That is the most important thing.

55
00:11:36.917 --> 00:11:50.606
Now, we tend to think the spike, that is the glamour thing. That is what really wins games. No, it is the down and dirty back row scrambling. And so we compare that to a father's character.

56
00:11:50.606 --> 00:12:03.209
So the dig is a father's character. In the absence of doing the hard daily work on your character, you do not have much to build on on the rest of the game plan for your son.

57
00:12:03.209 --> 00:12:16.826
So the dig-set-spike principle for raising a son to manhood starts with the dig: a father's character. It is a father's character. Now you can raise a son, but your goal is to raise a man.

58
00:12:16.826 --> 00:12:32.688
That is what you are trying to do. And the goal is not just to get him through the teenage years, but to get him into feeling comfortable, to understand what the calling of manhood is. And that is what we are all about.

59
00:12:32.688 --> 00:12:35.512
So it starts with a father's character.

60
00:12:35.512 --> 00:12:45.290
I want you guys to know that your tone is so important. Do not ever underestimate the supreme value of dad because you are so critical.

61
00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:59.703
You ought to see yourself as given by God this very exalted role with huge upside opportunity for you. It is important to understand that a dad's character actually sets the atmosphere of the family.

62
00:12:59.703 --> 00:13:11.871
The atmosphere of the family. So if the family is chilly, people are cool to each other in the family, look in the mirror, dad, because they are probably following you.

63
00:13:11.871 --> 00:13:23.387
If there is no emotion, if there is no touching, if there is no love, if there is no warmth, that is probably coming from the way you are relating to everyone.

64
00:13:23.387 --> 00:13:32.585
If the family is combative, lots of arguing, lots of tension, that is probably coming from you. It is probably coming from you.

65
00:13:32.585 --> 00:13:50.041
If your home is boring, like if you walk in the door of your home from an exciting life in your career where people respect you and honor you and you are going all day and it is really cool and you feel like, man, that is my world out there.

66
00:13:50.041 --> 00:14:05.541
Or maybe it is your hobbies. Like Saturday morning, sitting around the breakfast table eating pancakes or waffles with my kids when I could be on the golf course. You have got to be kidding me. This is boring here.

67
00:14:05.541 --> 00:14:19.375
What you need to understand is your sons will feel that way. And as soon as they get to an age, they want to get out of this boring house just like dad. And some of you wonder, what happened?

68
00:14:19.375 --> 00:14:30.022
My son does not want to be home. He does not want to be around me. As soon as you get freedom, he is gone. Sometimes that comes from you. From you.

69
00:14:30.022 --> 00:14:38.786
So you have the ability to set the atmosphere to bring laughter, to bring joy, to bring enthusiasm, and bring excitement.

70
00:14:38.786 --> 00:14:53.344
Your family needs to hear you tell them how much you love them, how much you enjoy spending time with them, how much you want to be together as a family. That creates a positive atmosphere in the home.

71
00:14:53.344 --> 00:15:07.975
If your kids see you hugging your wife, kissing your wife, complimenting your wife, it sets them up for comfortability in love in relationships. And so you have an opportunity to make a big, big impact.

72
00:15:07.975 --> 00:15:16.666
Your character also lays the groundwork for the aspirations that you have for your sons. Sons drink in your aspirations.

73
00:15:16.666 --> 00:15:28.906
And so if your aspirations are athletics, your sons will typically, not always, typically be all into athletics. If it is into politics, your kids will be into politics.

74
00:15:28.906 --> 00:15:37.163
If your aspirations are around your job or making money, your kids will typically aspire to those kinds of things.

75
00:15:37.163 --> 00:16:00.848
And so when it comes to worship, if your kids see you as their leader finding any excuse you can not to be involved, or every week on the way home from church you are criticizing what happened there, or they sit beside you in worship and you are like, I am a man, I do not sing karaoke and I am certainly not singing in church.

76
00:16:00.848 --> 00:16:15.986
What they do is they pick that up from you. If every time your kids catch you in the morning reading the Wall Street Journal instead of the word of God, what do they pick up from you? That is their aspiration.

77
00:16:15.986 --> 00:16:21.056
So your spiritual interest will also color the interest of their kids.

78
00:16:21.056 --> 00:16:37.715
One of the things that my dad did that was one of the best things he did for me: I would get up in the morning to get ready for school and when I pass my dad's bedroom every morning, my dad has his Bible open, reading and praying.

79
00:16:37.715 --> 00:16:53.360
Now, my dad was not a pastor. My dad was a State Farm insurance salesman. But every morning, he was reading his Bible and praying. That made a huge impact on me. Why is he doing that? Why is that so important to him?

80
00:16:53.360 --> 00:17:02.703
What is in there? It made me aspire to want to understand. And so what you aspire to, your family will tend to aspire to as well.

81
00:17:02.703 --> 00:17:18.275
So here is one of the really cool things. Fathers have been given a gift by God that we kindly refer to as father power. And the father power is this. Your kids come out of the womb and they love you. They love dad.

82
00:17:18.275 --> 00:17:34.210
They want to be with you. They want your approval. They will mimic you. They will follow you around. Whatever you think is fun, they think is fun. And in the early years, that father power is deeply embedded in children.

83
00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:45.436
I think God actually created us that way. That is why we have phrases like he is a chip off the old block or the apple does not fall too far from the tree.

84
00:17:45.436 --> 00:18:02.312
So what happens particularly with sons is if that father power is leveraged to draw the son in, giving your son time, attention, not just doing his activities but doing him, being with him, knowing him, speaking into him, loving him.

85
00:18:02.312 --> 00:18:06.295
If that happens, the son feels affirmed, feels blessed.

86
00:18:06.295 --> 00:18:23.099
If dad is too busy, dad has got too many priorities, dad does not feel like he can relate to his son, son gets brushed off, the kid will keep coming back and keep coming back and keep coming back because father power is so powerful.

87
00:18:23.099 --> 00:18:37.005
The problem is that as a child grows up, particularly boys, if dad does not have time or interest in their son, that love turns into pain. And in the teenage years, that pain turns into anger.

88
00:18:37.005 --> 00:18:50.622
And some of you know exactly what I am talking about because there was a point somewhere along the way where you got angry at dad. You might not have known why, but you got angry with dad.

89
00:18:50.622 --> 00:19:05.108
Some of you guys are dealing with sons right now that are angry with you. And you are thinking, where did all that anger come from? And the anger does not necessarily come from you abusing your child.

90
00:19:05.108 --> 00:19:17.711
It does not necessarily come from you being mean-spirited to your child. Oftentimes, it just comes out of the hurt that your child experienced because you were not investing.

91
00:19:17.711 --> 00:19:33.573
And so we challenge you guys, especially young dads, grandfathers, teach this to your sons so that they will love their kids. Leverage that father power. Use it when the kids are young. Build deep bonds with your child.

92
00:19:33.573 --> 00:19:48.421
If you will do that, they get your character and they will embrace you. And the relationship gives you an opportunity to build in their lives rather than the tension that often comes by failing to do that.

93
00:19:48.421 --> 00:20:01.820
And failing to do that does not mean you do not love your son. Oftentimes it is just neglect. Like I just got other things in my life that I got to get done and I do not think about it.

94
00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:10.439
That is why having a plan is so critical. The power of father is beyond imagination. It is beyond what you can imagine.

95
00:20:10.439 --> 00:20:18.696
So I just want you to know you have been gifted with something so incredible and you want to take advantage of it.

96
00:20:18.696 --> 00:20:29.053
There was a story in Reader's Digest a number of years ago called "The Catch of a Lifetime." Really interesting story about a father and a son.

97
00:20:29.053 --> 00:20:42.525
The one who is writing it is a 45-year-old man and he is writing about an experience he had when he was 11. And this was back when places had actual fishing seasons, like hunting season.

98
00:20:42.525 --> 00:20:57.590
And so it was 10 o'clock in the evening, bass season started at midnight, and the son and the dad get to the cabin on the lake and they pull in and the son says to the dad, "Hey, can I go down there and cast?

99
00:20:57.590 --> 00:21:13.235
I want to be ready. Fishing season starts in two hours. I want to be ready. Can I go down there and cast?" And so the dad says, "Sure." So the son runs down the dock, beautiful moon in the sky over the pristine lake.

100
00:21:13.235 --> 00:21:28.880
He runs out on the dock. He gets out his fishing rod and casts out there and he is reeling it back in. He catches a few sunfish and he is throwing them back and having a good time. And the dad walks down on the dock.

101
00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:43.656
Now keep in mind it is two hours from fishing season starting. Dad walks down on the dock. He says, "Dad, dad, dad, can I put a lure on here?" And his dad said, "Well, you know, you cannot catch anything.

102
00:21:43.656 --> 00:21:46.915
It is not fishing." "I just want to practice.

103
00:21:46.915 --> 00:22:04.443
I want to be ready." So sure enough, he puts a lure on there and he casts and he is reeling it in and he casts, he is reeling it in and all of a sudden he felt that tug and then that, and he sets the hook and for the next 20 minutes it is on.

104
00:22:04.443 --> 00:22:17.408
It is play and reel and play and reel and play and reel and it is what seemed like a lifetime. They finally pull up to the dock, the biggest bass either one of them had ever seen.

105
00:22:17.408 --> 00:22:27.983
And the dad reaches down and grabs the gills of this largemouth bass, pulls it up, and they are standing looking at it with their mouth wide open.

106
00:22:27.983 --> 00:22:38.702
And the dad says to the son, "You know, we got to release this fish." And the son says to the dad, "But dad, fishing season, it starts in two hours.

107
00:22:38.702 --> 00:22:54.057
You cannot let my fish go." And so they stand there looking at each other and there is no sound on the lake. There is nobody out on any of the docks. There is nobody looking. It is just the dad and the son there.

108
00:22:54.057 --> 00:23:12.382
And the dad says, "But son, the law is we cannot catch this fish for another two hours." And the son says, "Oh, dad, it is not a big deal." And the dad says, "Yeah, actually it is." And he unhooked the hook and released the bass, and the bass swam away.

109
00:23:12.382 --> 00:23:27.085
In writing this story, 34 years later, the son said that incident in my life came and went and I grew up and I lived on and it was no big deal, but I think about it often. He says, now I am an architect.

110
00:23:27.085 --> 00:23:44.396
I run my own firm and every day I have the opportunity to make a decision as to whether I am going to pad my estimates, whether I am going to cut corners on my work, whether I am going to work with subs that I know will do an inferior job.

111
00:23:44.396 --> 00:23:48.090
I have got opportunity to do that every single day.

112
00:23:48.090 --> 00:24:08.805
And every time I am tempted to cut corners, I think back to that day standing there with my dad on the dock looking at the largest fish I had ever seen up until that point and since that time and my dad reminding me that ethics are easy to understand, but they are real hard to execute.

113
00:24:08.805 --> 00:24:15.903
And if you cannot be honest in the little things, why would you not cut corners in the big things?

114
00:24:15.903 --> 00:24:29.809
And daily I make decisions that I will not sacrifice my ethics for a fish and I will not sacrifice my ethics for a dollar. Some of you are saying like that is just crazy. What is the big deal?

115
00:24:29.809 --> 00:24:35.893
See, that is the challenge for men. What is the big deal? And here is what we think.

116
00:24:35.893 --> 00:24:49.365
We think that we can go through our lives and we will compromise on the little things over and over again, but when it comes to something big, that is when we are going to hold the line.

117
00:24:49.365 --> 00:25:00.447
And what we do not realize is that if you cannot do the right thing with a fish, why do you think that you would do the right thing with a $100,000 deal?

118
00:25:00.447 --> 00:25:05.589
I mean, one is certainly much more important than the other one, right?

119
00:25:05.589 --> 00:25:16.453
So, if you do not have the ability to do the right thing, then you do not have the ability to do the right thing when it comes to the big ones either.

120
00:25:16.453 --> 00:25:26.231
And that is why what dad teaches his children has a huge bearing on the rest of life. And so, the dig, that is the father is character.

121
00:25:26.231 --> 00:25:40.572
Now the set in volleyball is when you receive the dig or the pass and you put it in scoring position. Okay. So what is that in the life of the father? The set is a father's training and instruction.

122
00:25:40.572 --> 00:25:50.277
So setting your son up to score, setting your son up to have a great life, it comes from secondly a father's training and instruction.

123
00:25:50.277 --> 00:26:04.835
Character is what your son observes in your behavior and your ethics and your morals and your principles and your attitude and your lifestyle. But here is where we get to words. Training gets to words.

124
00:26:04.835 --> 00:26:08.818
And you have three treasures to pass along to your son.

125
00:26:08.818 --> 00:26:19.320
The first one is a manhood vision. And that is what we have been learning, right? A real man does what? What are the four things a real man does?

126
00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:33.951
He rejects passivity, accepts responsibility, leads courageously, expects the greater reward, God's reward. Taking those concepts, incorporating them into your life, and then teaching them to your sons.

127
00:26:33.951 --> 00:26:42.787
We will talk more about how you go about teaching that in a few minutes, but that is the big idea for your manhood vision.

128
00:26:42.787 --> 00:26:58.577
The second treasure that you have is a code of conduct. The challenge is what are the specific ethics that characterize your family that you are regularly teaching to your sons that you associate with your family name?

129
00:26:58.577 --> 00:27:12.918
When you say I am a Hawks, what does that mean? Can you identify specific behaviors that you emphasize? And some would say, well, you know, do the right thing all the time, like do everything right.

130
00:27:12.918 --> 00:27:26.680
Well, oftentimes it is a lot more helpful to focus on four, five or six and reinforce those to become the bedrock for what a child can get. In 1998, I did a series called Real Family Values.

131
00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:49.930
You can go on our website on our media player and you can listen to the series, but where we basically talk through how do you work with your wife to actually establish a set of conduct or values for your family and then how do you begin to reinforce those, teach them to your children and to begin to put those in place.

132
00:27:49.930 --> 00:28:03.619
I am talking about things like courage. I am talking about things like faith, like obedience, like honesty, like hard work, relational integrity. Those kinds of things that you would adopt.

133
00:28:03.619 --> 00:28:12.093
And I would challenge every family to have four, five, six of those things that define you that you keep reinforcing.

134
00:28:12.093 --> 00:28:24.044
Now, here is one of the things that is really important. Whatever the values are of your family, anchor them in the word. And here is why that is really significant.

135
00:28:24.044 --> 00:28:33.242
What you do not want your kids to do is grow up thinking, well, dad said this. Dad said this. Dad thinks this. Dad thinks this.

136
00:28:33.242 --> 00:28:40.557
What you want your kids to think is that there is actually something bigger than dad that guides dad.

137
00:28:40.557 --> 00:28:53.015
So that when you fail your children in your humanity, which we all do, they actually realize that you are an imperfect man, but you are taking your cues from a perfect God.

138
00:28:53.015 --> 00:29:08.732
And so tether your characteristics or your family values. Tether those to things in the Bible that you can use and lean on along the way. In the 1950s, people threw out the Bible but kept the principles and practices.

139
00:29:08.732 --> 00:29:23.870
In the 1960s, you had a generation of kids who came along and said, why would we maintain the principles when there is no authority behind those? And that is what has happened in our culture all along the way.

140
00:29:23.870 --> 00:29:34.300
So, make sure that there is a biblical authority behind each of the values that you embrace. They are very, very important and very significant.

141
00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:47.265
I want you to see the value of talking to your sons and teaching them. In Proverbs chapter 4, Solomon is writing about his relationship with his dad and what his dad did with him.

142
00:29:47.265 --> 00:30:03.127
Imagine this conversation taking place with your father. And if you are a father of sons, your sons. What would this experience be like? "Listen, my sons, to a father's instruction. Pay attention and gain understanding.

143
00:30:03.127 --> 00:30:15.078
I give you sound learning. Do not forsake my teaching." That is David speaking. And that is Solomon hearing that from his dad. Listen to what Solomon goes on to say.

144
00:30:15.078 --> 00:30:30.288
He says, "When I was a boy in my father's house, still tender and an only child of my mother, he taught me and said, Lay hold of my words with all your heart. Keep my commandments and you will live. Get wisdom.

145
00:30:30.288 --> 00:30:38.255
Get understanding. Do not forget my words or swerve from them. Do not forsake wisdom and she will protect you.

146
00:30:38.255 --> 00:30:49.192
Love her and she will watch over you." Now, are you imagining yourself with your dad having these kinds of conversations on a regular basis growing up?

147
00:30:49.192 --> 00:31:04.764
Or if you are the father of sons, you having these kinds of conversations with your child. David goes on talking to Solomon, "Wisdom is supreme. Therefore, get wisdom. Though it cost all you have, get understanding.

148
00:31:04.764 --> 00:31:16.135
Esteem her and she will exalt you. Embrace her and she will honor you. She will set a garland of grace on your head and present you with a crown of splendor.

149
00:31:16.135 --> 00:31:29.969
Listen, my son, accept what I say, and the years of your life will be many. I guide you in the way of wisdom and lead you along straight paths. When you walk, your steps will not be hampered.

150
00:31:29.969 --> 00:31:45.107
When you run, you will not stumble. Hold on to instruction. Do not let it go. Guard it well, for it is your life." I would suggest that next to none of us in this room had these kind of conversations with dad.

151
00:31:45.107 --> 00:32:00.100
But this is what we are called to do. Pass along to our sons a code of conduct. Teach them wisdom. Explain to them the path. In fact, most societies value fathers teaching their sons, instructing their sons.

152
00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:07.705
Part of being a father is to pass along wisdom. Do you have a code of conduct you are teaching your sons?

153
00:32:07.705 --> 00:32:16.541
The third treasure that you have to offer is a transcendent cause. The word transcendent means to go beyond. To go beyond.

154
00:32:16.541 --> 00:32:25.232
And we said from the start, if your cause never exceeds the borders of yourself, in time you become a very small person.

155
00:32:25.232 --> 00:32:41.891
If it is only about my happiness, if it is only about my hobbies, if it is only about my money, if it is only about my fun, if it is only about how I look, if it is only about my health, it is only about my desires or what I like.

156
00:32:41.891 --> 00:32:56.739
If that is true of you, over time, you become a very small person. Where we really grow and excel is when we have got something bigger than us that we are living for that calls forth something significant.

157
00:32:56.739 --> 00:33:12.311
That is why you see guys that maybe they go to college for a little while and they think, this is not for me. And then later on they get an interest in life and they go, wow, I am really excited about that interest.

158
00:33:12.311 --> 00:33:28.101
And they go back to school and they get straight As on everything. Where is that coming from? Well, they found something bigger and now they are motivated. You get smarter when you have a cause that is bigger than you.

159
00:33:28.101 --> 00:33:44.036
You grow in character when you have a cause that is bigger than you. Your self-discipline moves out in a bigger way when there is something more important. And so therefore, having a transcendent cause is so significant.

160
00:33:44.036 --> 00:33:49.468
And I would challenge you guys to consider the most important cause of all.

161
00:33:49.468 --> 00:34:01.636
Jesus said, "But seek first my kingdom and my righteousness and all these other things will be taken care of." I think Jesus Christ is the greatest cause on the planet.

162
00:34:01.636 --> 00:34:14.384
And if you live for the righteousness of Jesus Christ and seeing his kingdom expanded, it will call forth everything in you. And God will begin to grow you in magnificent ways.

163
00:34:14.384 --> 00:34:22.930
And so challenge your sons to have a transcendent cause, something bigger than them, something bigger than themselves.

164
00:34:22.930 --> 00:34:31.259
So the set is a father's training and instruction. So the dig is down and dirty. That is the day-to-day activities.

165
00:34:31.259 --> 00:34:46.179
The set is putting your sons in a position to score by giving them insight, information, teaching them, explaining the world to them, giving them something to live for, challenging them to something bigger.

166
00:34:46.179 --> 00:34:56.029
And then the final step in the process is the spike. Like how do we drive this home in such a dramatic way that our sons really succeed?

167
00:34:56.029 --> 00:35:11.819
And here is another area where we in America struggle a lot because we just do not have this built into our culture. The spike is a father's ceremonies. A father's ceremonies. How do you highlight these values in life?

168
00:35:11.819 --> 00:35:26.450
We tend to put ceremonies with things that are important. And so, high school graduation, we have a ceremony. College graduation, we have a ceremony. When people are getting married, we have ceremonies.

169
00:35:26.450 --> 00:35:35.793
When people pass from this life, we have a ceremony. We call that a funeral. It is a time when we say, this was really important.

170
00:35:35.793 --> 00:35:48.685
So what ceremonies do we have to reinforce for men the big important nature of being a man, being manhood. And so we are going to challenge you a little bit about how to do this.

171
00:35:48.685 --> 00:36:02.374
The lack of passages for sons leads people in all kinds of different directions. And so you will find initiation rites in different groups all around the world and even some in our culture.

172
00:36:02.374 --> 00:36:18.236
And some of them are really negative, like gang initiation. You have to kill somebody to get in a gang. What is that? It is a rite of passage. It is a ceremony. So, you steal something to be able to be part of the gang.

173
00:36:18.236 --> 00:36:33.229
In the fraternity, you have to get hazed and do some pretty crazy things before you can become part of the fraternity. Those kinds of things substitute oftentimes for real, legitimate, meaningful ceremonies.

174
00:36:33.229 --> 00:36:40.906
What did your dad do to memorialize or to put in place an understanding that you have passed into manhood?

175
00:36:40.906 --> 00:36:56.696
Here is what my dad did. I graduated from college and I was working in Dallas getting ready to go to seminary and seminary was really expensive and so I am working for a remodeler but I just could not get enough hours.

176
00:36:56.696 --> 00:37:09.951
I mean it was good pay but I just could not get enough hours. So I got my car and drove to Clinton, Oklahoma. It was back during the oil boom and I got a room would be the wrong word.

177
00:37:09.951 --> 00:37:25.668
A guy I knew had a 22-foot travel trailer and for $125 a month he was renting it out to five other guys and myself and so that we were taking turns sleeping. Fortunately I got on nights and they were all working days.

178
00:37:25.668 --> 00:37:41.530
So I actually got a place to sleep until it got to be 105 degrees at noon and then I could not sleep anymore. But anyway. Anybody in here a roughneck? Got some roughnecks in here. Yeah. One. Okay. You know, right? Okay.

179
00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:44.065
So I have no experience whatsoever.

180
00:37:44.065 --> 00:38:07.315
I am pulling up to these oil rigs, walking up on the platform talking to the driller, and everyone I talked to, they said, "Do you have any experience?" And I say, "No." And they say, "Yeah, we do not need anybody without experience." Well, finally, I walk up on a rig and the driller says, "Yeah, we do not have anybody.

181
00:38:07.315 --> 00:38:22.308
We will get you started here." So, I found out that I was a worm at that point in time. That is what they call people that do not have any experience that is working on oil derrick. And it is real hard work.

182
00:38:22.308 --> 00:38:33.462
It is real dangerous. It is real dangerous. Had chains break right next to my face. I had people drop heavy things on my head from the top of the derrick.

183
00:38:33.462 --> 00:38:47.948
I was standing up on top of the derrick with a 16-pound hammer one day trying to get the bails loose because they had hung them up in the top of the draw works. I mean, just all kinds of weird things.

184
00:38:47.948 --> 00:39:00.406
But anyway, the money was great and a lot of it was cash. So, I needed the money for seminary. Well, I do not know if I am doing the right thing. I go back. I go into town.

185
00:39:00.406 --> 00:39:14.964
I put a quarter in a pay phone and I call my dad in Michigan and I tell my dad what the conditions are and what is going on and what I am doing and all this kind of stuff. And here is what my dad said.

186
00:39:14.964 --> 00:39:26.118
He said, "Well, you are there and I do not know what this thing is like. And so, I guess it is up to you. You need to use your best judgment. I trust you.

187
00:39:26.118 --> 00:39:38.648
So, whatever you think, that is good." I hung up the phone and I got in my car and drove back to the oil rig and I thought to myself, I think my dad just told me I am a man.

188
00:39:38.648 --> 00:39:54.583
I think he just said, "Son, I believe that you have got this." And that was a great moment. I am talking on a pay phone in Oklahoma to a guy in Michigan and that is the best we could do. You guys can do better than that.

189
00:39:54.583 --> 00:40:07.331
And so that is why we are talking about what are the ceremonies that you can put in place where your son clearly, clearly understands what it is that we are talking about here.

190
00:40:07.331 --> 00:40:14.501
So lack of passages for sons. The ceremony process is built on the knight and his ceremony process.

191
00:40:14.501 --> 00:40:24.206
Interestingly enough, there was a very clear way that the knights came into their role of nobility and their honor codes and so forth.

192
00:40:24.206 --> 00:40:37.750
And Robert Lewis has done a fantastic job in a book called *Raising a Modern-Day Knight.* And I would encourage all of you guys to get this, *Raising a Modern-Day Knight* by Robert Lewis.

193
00:40:37.750 --> 00:40:49.049
And he walks through what he did with his sons, he and a group of guys, and walks through the ceremony. So, we are not going to spend a lot of time on that.

194
00:40:49.049 --> 00:40:56.871
We are actually going to give you a little bit of information about what we have available here to help you.

195
00:40:56.871 --> 00:41:10.705
But just a few characteristics of memorable ceremonies. The first one is memorable ceremonies are costly. They cost you time. They cost you effort. They cost you an investment with other men.

196
00:41:10.705 --> 00:41:25.553
If you really want your sons to get the value of what this is that they are doing, just like a wedding costs money, if you want to bring it to a level of like this really is meaningful. So they are costly.

197
00:41:25.553 --> 00:41:37.938
Secondly, memorable ceremonies are ones that ascribe value. In other words, it is not just a ceremony for ceremony's sake, but everything in the ceremony is tied to value.

198
00:41:37.938 --> 00:41:51.555
So when my oldest son and his friends, we had four or five guys and five dads. When they turned 13, we had a ceremony for them. We took them away to a camp for a Friday night and Saturday.

199
00:41:51.555 --> 00:42:07.055
And we brought the grandfathers in. So we had three generations, paid for the grandfathers to come in, and then we did some things to try to help them understand the magnitude of what we are doing. One of them, Dr.

200
00:42:07.055 --> 00:42:23.641
John Morrow was one of the dads that was part of the deal, and John on Friday night taught the kids how to find the north star in the sky and kind of walk through how you discover where the north star is and then ascribe to that.

201
00:42:23.641 --> 00:42:34.940
That is what the Bible is for you. It is your north star. It is what you go back to. When you do not know how to navigate life, that is what you go back to.

202
00:42:34.940 --> 00:42:56.017
And one of the other things that we did to kind of get our sons thinking correctly about life is we had a little quarter-mile track that was out there in the park and we gave them all backpacks and they put the backpacks on and we had them run around the quarter mile with an empty backpack.

203
00:42:56.017 --> 00:43:06.519
And when they finished we said, well, that is kind of where you guys are right now in middle school. Backpack is pretty light, pretty easy to do.

204
00:43:06.519 --> 00:43:15.572
We poured a couple rocks in the backpack and we said, okay, now moving into high school, this is going to be what it is like.

205
00:43:15.572 --> 00:43:29.334
And they had to run around the track with a little weight in their back because you got more responsibilities. Classes are going to be harder. They actually make a difference in your future.

206
00:43:29.334 --> 00:43:45.124
But the next phase, you are going to go off to college. We poured a few more rocks in their backpack and they had to run. And we explained the responsibilities of college. And then you are going to get out on your own.

207
00:43:45.124 --> 00:43:57.872
You are going to get a job. We poured a few more rocks in. They ran again. And then you are going to get married and start having kids. And we poured a whole bunch of rocks in.

208
00:43:57.872 --> 00:44:06.056
And by now they are starting to realize like we are teaching you growing levels of responsibility are hard to do.

209
00:44:06.056 --> 00:44:16.775
But when we finally finished up, the dads all put the backpacks on and we ran the quarter mile side by side with our sons and we told our sons this.

210
00:44:16.775 --> 00:44:24.887
As you go through life and responsibilities get heavy, you need to know that we will always be here to help you.

211
00:44:24.887 --> 00:44:36.403
Your fathers and these other men will always be here to help you and you guys will be here to help each other. What are we teaching them? Do not go this alone.

212
00:44:36.403 --> 00:44:41.473
And so the ceremonies: costly, invest time, but connect them to value.

213
00:44:41.473 --> 00:44:54.872
Letter C. Memorable ceremonies are the ones that employ symbols. We think about in terms of rings at weddings, a diploma at graduation. What are the symbols that symbolize these things?

214
00:44:54.872 --> 00:45:09.503
In the knight ceremony that Robert Lewis does, swords and crests, family crests and so forth are part of what they encourage. And then finally, letter D, memorable ceremonies empower a life with vision.

215
00:45:09.503 --> 00:45:21.743
Each step of the way, you are saying, okay, your life, son, up to this point looked like this. Now, after this ceremony, here is what your life looks like going forward.

216
00:45:21.743 --> 00:45:37.605
And then we put on here the four stages that Robert Lewis does in his book, but rather than me continuing, the thing that we offer here to get you started is called Journey to Manhood. And that is going to be available.

217
00:45:37.605 --> 00:45:47.021
So, I am going to have Nate come up as we close the session and give you a little bit more information about what that looks like.

218
00:45:47.021 --> 00:46:02.231
And for those of you who are beyond Journey to Manhood, I really encourage you to get this book, read it over. It is a quick read. It will help you even if your sons are teenagers in college, prior to marriage.

219
00:46:02.231 --> 00:46:09.000
All this is really helpful to consider and work on together. So, let me turn it over to Nate.
